extends /views/templates/layouts/_base

append variables
	- pagetitle = "Panel | Examples | Static"

block content
	.lane
		.container
			.page-header
				h1 Static
				p.lead A panel that stops being a disclosure when there is room for it.


	+section("Live demo")

		p Drag the handle on the right, or give it focus and use the arrow keys. Above #[code 260px] the panel is #[strong static]: it is simply open, and the button is gone. Below that it is a disclosure again, and the button comes back.

		p The markup underneath is live. Watch #[code aria-expanded] and #[code inert] disappear as #[code data-ps-static] arrives, because that is what this option really is. It is not a layout trick, it is a change of what the thing #[em is].

		.card
			.static-demo
				.static-resizer
					#static-box.static-box
						#static-panel(data-panel data-panel-axis="horizontal" data-panel-align="start")
							.panel-wrapper(role="navigation" aria-label="Demo")
								p Sidebar
						.demo-body
							button(aria-controls="static-panel" aria-expanded="false") Menu
							p Content

					#static-handle.static-handle(role="separator" aria-orientation="vertical" aria-label="Resize the demo" tabindex="0")

				p.static-readout#static-readout Measuring…

			.code-block.static-markup
				.code-header
					| Live HTML
				pre
					code.live-code#static-markup


	+section("What a screen reader is told")

		p If a developer tries to force a drawer to look permanent with CSS alone, that will not work. The library still thinks it is a closed disclosure, so the markup keeps saying so: #[code inert] hides the sidebar everyone can see, and #[code aria-expanded="false"] claims a button can expand something that is already expanded. The page looks right and reads wrong.

		p Static is not "open the panel", it is "stop being a disclosure". The JavaScript takes #[code inert] off the panel and #[strong removes] #[code aria-expanded] from the trigger. Not set to #[code true]: gone, because there is nothing left to expand. The stylesheet then hides that trigger, since a button that can do nothing should not hold a tab stop. Go back the other way and the panel lands closed with both attributes restored.

		p Those are two separate layers, on purpose. Hiding the button is #[strong CSS], and you can take it back with #[code display: revert] to keep the button and give it another job. Removing #[code aria-expanded] is #[strong JavaScript], and it is not optional: a button you chose to keep must not go on claiming it expands a panel that cannot collapse, and your stylesheet may not load ours at all.

		p #[code aria-controls] goes as well, for the same reason. A button you keep and repurpose must not still say it controls this panel. So a static panel's trigger claims nothing about it at all: it is hidden, and if you unhide it, it is simply a button.

		p Which leaves the panel needing another way to recognise its own button, since #[code aria-controls] is exactly how it used to find one. So on its way into static it stamps a hook first: #[code data-ps-for="panel-id"]. That, and not the ARIA, is how a static panel finds its triggers, which is how it can give #[code aria-controls] back when it becomes a disclosure again.

		p You never write #[code data-ps-for] yourself, and it lives exactly as long as #[code aria-controls] is away: it arrives as the panel goes static and leaves as soon as #[code aria-controls] comes back. An ordinary panel never carries one. But if you go looking for the trigger of a static panel in your own code, that is what to look for, because #[code aria-controls] will not be there.

		p #[code setStatic()] does all of the above when the breakpoint is not a viewport media query.

		h3 Do not name the panel after its button

		p If a panel can go static, do not label it with #[code aria-labelledby] pointing at its own trigger, which is the usual accordion pattern. The name survives (a name read through #[code aria-labelledby] still comes off an element that CSS has hidden, the one place hidden text counts), so nothing breaks. But the panel ends up named after a button that is no longer on the page.

		p Give it a name of its own instead, with #[code aria-label], or point #[code aria-labelledby] at a heading #[em inside] the panel, which is content that stays. Panel never touches the label itself: stripping it would leave the panel with no name at all, which is worse than a stale one. With #[code debug] on, it says so in the console.


	+section("How it is driven")

		h3 With a breakpoint

		p This is what you want almost every time. Put the media query in the markup and the library watches it for you, with no JavaScript of your own at all:

		+codeblock('HTML').
			<div data-panel data-panel-static="(min-width: 769px)">

		p The breakpoint then lives in exactly one place. Your CSS does not repeat it, because the library marks the panel and its trigger with #[code data-ps-static] while the query matches, and you can style off that.

		h3 With setStatic()

		p The demo above cannot use a breakpoint. #[code static] takes a media query, and a media query only ever sees the #[strong viewport], not a box on the page. A container query cannot help either: CSS never hands those to JavaScript, and there is no #[code matchContainer()].

		p So you watch whatever you like and tell the panel what it is:

		+codeblock('JavaScript').
			const box = document.getElementById('static-box');
			const panel = document.getElementById('static-panel').panel;

			new ResizeObserver(([entry]) => {
				panel.setStatic(entry.contentRect.width >= 260);
			}).observe(box);

		p Crossing the threshold never animates, so dragging the handle does not look like the panel opening and closing over and over. And #[code setStatic()] with the state it is already in does nothing, so calling it on every resize frame is fine.

		p Reach for it only when the thing you are reacting to is something the library cannot see for itself: a container query, your own breakpoint system, a feature flag.


	+section("Reading the state")

		p #[code panel.isStatic] tells you where it stands, and #[code panel:staticchange] fires on every switch, with #[code detail.static]. That event is what the markup above listens to.

		+codeblock('JavaScript').
			panel.element.addEventListener('panel:staticchange', (e) => {
				console.log(e.detail.static ? 'plain content now' : 'a disclosure again');
			});

		.callout
			+warning()
			p A static panel is #[strong not] an open panel. It has no open or closed state at all, so #[code open()], #[code close()] and #[code toggle()] do nothing, and #[code persist] and #[code deepLink] write nothing. When it becomes a disclosure again it starts as being #[strong closed], because a panel that reappears already open over your content is never what you want.


append scripts
	if isProd
		script(src=`${basePath}lib/panelset.js` vite-ignore)
		script.
			Panel.init('.docs-content [data-panel]');
		script(src=`${basePath}assets/scripts/example-static.js` vite-ignore)
	else
		script(type="module").
			import { Panel } from 'panelset';
			Panel.init('.docs-content [data-panel]');
		script(type="module" src="/src/assets/scripts/example-static.js")
